<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    The add button — an extended FAB: the Material "add" mark plus a label.

    WHY IT IS NOT A SINGLE <Button>
    A MAUI Button is text-and-image only. It has no content property (that belongs to
    TemplatedView, which Button is not) and therefore no ControlTemplate either, so neither a
    vector icon nor an icon-beside-a-label arrangement can be supplied from a Style. The
    markup therefore has to be siblings, which means the control's root can no longer be the
    Button — hence ContentView with a transparent Button laid over the pill. The same
    constraint is why the row's overflow mark is a Path under its button in CatalogRow.xaml;
    keep the two in step.

    WHY THERE IS A BUTTON AT ALL UNDER THE MARK
    The button is the control's whole input surface and the reason it is a control rather
    than a drawing. It supplies Command, CanExecute-driven disabling, keyboard activation,
    the automation role and the visual states — all platform behaviour. An earlier version
    was a Border carrying a TapGestureRecognizer for the command plus a
    PointerGestureRecognizer alongside it for the press state, and that made the button
    dead: TapGestureRecognizer *derives from* PointerGestureRecognizer, so the plain
    recognizer claimed the pointer sequence first, the tap never completed, and the command
    never ran. There is exactly one recognizer-free input element here — the Button — and it
    covers the pill, so nothing can intercept it.

    THE PRESS STATE
    The button is transparent at rest and paints only a translucent state layer when
    pressed, over the Primary fill of the Border beneath it. Material's feedback is a
    scrim, not a colour change; the previous fill swap to Tertiary plus a 0.94 shrink read
    as the button changing identity rather than being touched.

    THE MARK
    Data is a literal attribute, which is what makes it work at all: XAML runs a string
    through the geometry TypeConverter, so Path="M …" parses. Bind it instead and the
    loader is handed a string where a Geometry is expected and throws InvalidCastException.
    The glyph is Material's "add", on its native 24x24 viewBox, at Aspect=Uniform in a 24dp
    box — so it is drawn 1:1 with no scaling blur. It replaced the text character "+", which
    inherited its weight, optical size and baseline from the body font and therefore sat
    differently in the circle on every platform.
-->
<ContentView xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             xmlns:shapes="clr-namespace:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Shapes;assembly=Microsoft.Maui.Controls"
             x:Class="CafePosApp.Controls.FloatingActionButton"
             x:Name="Root">

    <Border Style="{StaticResource ExtendedFAB}">
        <!-- ColumnSpacing is the 8dp gap between mark and label; the 16dp side insets are
             Margins on the two children rather than Padding on this Grid, because the
             button below spans both columns and needs to reach the pill's rounded ends —
             padding here would shrink its touch area and square off the pressed layer.
             HeightRequest repeats the container height so the button's target is the full
             pill even before the Border has been measured. -->
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8" HeightRequest="56">
            <shapes:Path Grid.Column="0" Margin="16,0,0,0"
                        Style="{StaticResource ExtendedFABIcon}"
                        InputTransparent="True"
                        Data="M19,13h-6v6h-2v-6H5v-2h6V5h2v6h6v2z" />

            <!-- A forward reference inside the control's own namescope — the safe
                 direction, the same one CatalogRow uses. -->
            <Label Grid.Column="1" Margin="0,0,16,0"
                   Style="{StaticResource ExtendedFABLabel}"
                   Text="{Binding Label, Source={x:Reference Root}}" />

            <!-- Last child, so the pressed state layer lands on top of the mark and the
                 label rather than under them. -->
            <Button x:Name="TouchTarget" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                    Style="{StaticResource FabTouchTarget}" />
        </Grid>
    </Border>
</ContentView>